# Resource metadata declaration coupling: bounded proposal

Current definition.bend is an affine wrapper around arbitrary Plan:Type with trusted supplied resource metadata. Source witness5009 passed feasibility only. It cannot derive requirements from the opaque Plan, and does not prove that supplied metadata matches its actual read declarations. Full automatic declaration coupling remains #54.

Pinned Inspector.make91–128 delegates to System.System, returns kind/name/system/read and the exact system.requirements array. System.collectSystemRequirements1018–1031 visits services then resources then machine/transition categories; Requirement.collect49–68 deduplicates descriptor keys in first-occurrence order. Query declarations do not contribute requirements. System.readResource142–168 has one read mode, not an optional-access helper: absence of a runtime value does not remove its declared requirement. Direct Runtime.inspect1695 invokes the callback without Schedule availability preflight; Runtime.tryRunSchedule1674 is a different API.

The reference uses actual bound Game.Inspector for empty/query-only/resource-absent/resource-present/alias declarations. The absent/present Runtime.make setups never call runtime.inspect. Repeated metadata reads observe the same requirement array/read function without executing it. The trusted same-display-name/distinct-ID case uses low-level Inspector.make only, on two same-kind/name descriptors outside schema registration, sharing the same interned key; it makes no claim about duplicate-schema-descriptor validation. Fixture numeric IDs are explicit one-to-one labels for actual descriptor symbol keys; same-key labels retain first occurrence; no distinct identity is asserted.

Next narrow coupling proposal for root interface review: a trusted resource declaration record carries existing nominal resource token plus display name and a closed typed projection grant. A resource-only named builder constructs BOTH the typed read Plan and ordered metadata from that same declaration input. Repeated aliases share the same nominal token; capability construction and metadata derivation reuse it. Explicit Ops types remain schema authoring declarations; this is not an erased dictionary, permissive Ops<I.Frame>, service grant, held-view or arbitrary descriptor validation policy. The current generic wrapper stays separately useful for arbitrary affine Plan owners, with its trusted-metadata limitation intact. No arbitrary Plan introspection is proposed.

Before claiming coupling, an independently authored consumer must read every actual declared resource through the produced Plan, compare every projected cell with metadata identity/order, reject undeclared/cross-schema/write authority with matched positives, and preserve entire affine owners on refusal/success. Owner-only tests use two actual Arrays plus the actual typed Inspector Plan, metadata read twice then return the original Plan owner. Names do not prove token identity; body/source inspection does not replace complete observations. The frozen Bend oracle includes every cell of both Arrays, not TS alias identity; the frozen TS oracle additionally reports actual callback identity and shared requirements array. Those host pointer facts are not inferred for Bend.

Exact current frozen cases: Workshop/Garden × empty, query-only (both required and optional component queries), one resource with runtime absent/present, aliases in ID order2 then1, same-kind/name constructors interned to one key labeled3 × first/repeat metadata reads. Full TS24 observations and independent Bend24 String lines are frozen in oracle.json before execution. Three static controls target nominal resource schema mismatch, duplicated Definition/Plan owner and Plan escape as String, with actual make/into_plan positive siblings. The proposed compiling ordering mutation reverses the complete deduplicated requirements; complete original oracle must reject actual semantic order while all owner/callback fields agree. No backend/static negative/mutant ran yet.

